Field Value RUles

1. A field value does not include leading or trailing whitespace [When a specific version of HTTP allows such whitespace to appear in a message, a field parsing implementation MUST exclude such whitespace prior to evaluating the field value]
2. Field values are usually constrained to the range of US-ASCII characters [USASCII]. 
3. Fields needing a greater range of characters can use an encoding, such as the one defined in [RFC8187 - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8187.html : It can be applied to any HTTP header field that uses the common "parameter" ("name=value") syntax]
		[RFC8187 use RFC6365 - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6365 to define "character encoding scheme" : AKA "character encoding", "charset", and "coded character set" : This is different from the MIME definition of "character encoding scheme"]
4. CR, LF, or NUL within a field value MUST either be rejected or replaced with SP
5. Field values containing other CTL characters are also invalid [ recipients MAY retain such characters when they appear within a safe context (e.g., an application-specific quoted string that will not be processed by any downstream HTTP parser) ]
6. Fields that only anticipate a single member as the field value are referred to as "singleton fields"
7. Fields that allow multiple members as the field value are referred to as "list-based fields"
8. Fields that expect to contain a comma within a member, such as within an HTTP-date or URI-reference element, ought to be defined with delimiters around that element to distinguish any comma within that data from potential list separators.
     [ Example-URIs: "http://example.com/a.html,foo", "http://without-a-comma.example.com/"]
Example-Dates: "Sat, 04 May 1996", "Wed, 14 Sep 2005"

Fields are sent and received within the header and trailer sections of messages 

Field sections are composed of any number of "field lines", each with a "field name" and a "field line value"
	
	if (Field name count == 1 in a section) 
		"field value" = "field line value"
	else if (field name > 1)
		"field value" = "field line value 1" + "," + "field line value 2" ...

Field Limits : No limits (ad hoc limitations on individual lengths are found in practice, often depending on the specific fields semantics)

Header Rules (RFC9110 5)
1. Host and Connection fields ought to be recognized by all HTTP implementations whether or not they advertise conformance with HTTP/1.1
2. (Other) recipients SHOULD ignore unrecognized header and trailer fields
3. Senders MUST NOT generate multiple field lines with the same name in a message unless fields definition allows multiple field line values to be recombined
4. Field value order must be preserved
5. A server MUST NOT apply a request to the target resource until it receives the entire request header section


